1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,827
<font color="#000000">Molecular modeling in quantum mechanics demands
models that respect physical laws,</font>

2
00:00:05,907 --> 00:00:11,433
<font color="#000000">namely invariance and equivariance to rotation and
translation of atomic coordinates.</font>

3
00:00:11,513 --> 00:00:13,609
<font color="#000000">This paper introduces GeoMFormer,</font>

4
00:00:13,689 --> 00:00:19,939
<font color="#000000">a general and flexible Transformer-based
architecture that learns both invariant and equivariant</font>

5
00:00:20,019 --> 00:00:22,181
<font color="#000000">molecular representations at once.</font>

6
00:00:22,261 --> 00:00:27,325
<font color="#000000">It uses two parallel Transformer streams, one for
each type of representation,</font>

7
00:00:27,405 --> 00:00:34,051
<font color="#000000">and bridges them with carefully designed
cross-attention modules so information flows between the two.</font>

8
00:00:34,131 --> 00:00:38,997
<font color="#000000">Many prior geometric models turn out to be special
cases of this framework,</font>

9
00:00:39,077 --> 00:00:44,800
<font color="#000000">and GeoMFormer sets new state-of-the-art results
across a wide range of molecular tasks.</font>

10
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,015
<font color="#000000">Deep learning has become a powerful tool for
molecular science,</font>

11
00:00:49,095 --> 00:00:55,602
<font color="#000000">predicting properties of molecules from their
three-dimensional coordinates and simulating how atoms move.</font>

12
00:00:55,682 --> 00:00:58,771
<font color="#000000">But these tasks impose strict physical constraints.</font>

13
00:00:58,851 --> 00:01:05,234
<font color="#000000">A model's prediction must transform correctly when
the input coordinate system is rotated or translated,</font>

14
00:01:05,314 --> 00:01:11,137
<font color="#000000">a requirement known as invariance for scalar
quantities and equivariance for vector quantities.</font>

15
00:01:11,217 --> 00:01:13,747
<font color="#000000">Existing methods handle these constraints,</font>

16
00:01:13,827 --> 00:01:16,916
<font color="#000000">but most are built on heuristic and costly modules,</font>

17
00:01:16,996 --> 00:01:23,068
<font color="#000000">and few offer a single general framework that
learns both invariant and equivariant representations</font>

18
00:01:23,148 --> 00:01:24,870
<font color="#000000">effectively at the same time.</font>

19
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:29,290
<font color="#000000">The trouble with existing geometric models is that
they are largely built by hand.</font>

20
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:33,410
<font color="#000000">Designers craft specialized equivariant modules
that are either expensive to scale or</font>

21
00:01:33,490 --> 00:01:37,530
<font color="#000000">so constrained that they sacrifice expressive power,</font>

22
00:01:37,610 --> 00:01:41,650
<font color="#000000">and the resulting architectures grow complex just
to guarantee the physical constraints.</font>

23
00:01:41,730 --> 00:01:45,770
<font color="#000000">More importantly,</font>

24
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:49,890
<font color="#000000">real applications increasingly demand a single
model that performs both invariant and</font>

25
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:54,010
<font color="#000000">equivariant prediction with strong accuracy.</font>

26
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:58,130
<font color="#000000">There is a clear need for a general, flexible
framework built on well-understood,</font>

27
00:01:58,210 --> 00:02:02,250
<font color="#000000">standard components rather than one-off heuristic modules.</font>

28
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:06,440
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

29
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:10,330
<font color="#000000">First, it introduces GeoMFormer,</font>

30
00:02:10,410 --> 00:02:14,220
<font color="#000000">a novel Transformer-based molecular model that
maintains two separate streams,</font>

31
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:18,110
<font color="#000000">one for invariant and one for equivariant representations,</font>

32
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:22,000
<font color="#000000">using only standard Transformer building blocks.</font>

33
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:25,890
<font color="#000000">Second, it designs cross-attention modules that
bridge these two streams,</font>

34
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:29,780
<font color="#000000">letting each draw on contextual information from
the other to enhance geometric modeling.</font>

35
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:33,670
<font color="#000000">Third,</font>

36
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:37,560
<font color="#000000">it shows that this framework is general enough that
many previously proposed architectures</font>

37
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:41,450
<font color="#000000">can be viewed as special instantiations of GeoMFormer,</font>

38
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:45,340
<font color="#000000">and it backs the design with strong empirical
results across a diverse set of molecular tasks.</font>

39
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:51,362
<font color="#000000">GeoMFormer keeps two representations for every
atom: an invariant feature vector and an</font>

40
00:02:51,442 --> 00:02:53,861
<font color="#000000">equivariant three-dimensional feature.</font>

41
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:57,281
<font color="#000000">These flow through two parallel Transformer streams.</font>

42
00:02:57,361 --> 00:03:02,609
<font color="#000000">Within each stream, a self-attention module first
mixes information across atoms.</font>

43
00:03:02,689 --> 00:03:04,385
<font color="#000000">For the equivariant stream,</font>

44
00:03:04,465 --> 00:03:10,238
<font color="#000000">standard attention is modified so the attention
score is computed by summing dot products</font>

45
00:03:10,318 --> 00:03:13,461
<font color="#000000">over the three-dimensional Query and Key vectors,</font>

46
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:15,960
<font color="#000000">which provably preserves equivariance.</font>

47
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:17,605
<font color="#000000">Then, the key innovation,</font>

48
00:03:17,685 --> 00:03:23,327
<font color="#000000">a cross-attention module lets each stream query the
other: the invariant stream attends</font>

49
00:03:23,407 --> 00:03:26,023
<font color="#000000">to the equivariant stream and vice versa,</font>

50
00:03:26,103 --> 00:03:29,049
<font color="#000000">fusing the two kinds of geometric information.</font>

51
00:03:29,129 --> 00:03:33,521
<font color="#000000">A feed-forward network completes each block, and
blocks are stacked.</font>

52
00:03:33,601 --> 00:03:38,717
<font color="#000000">Because the design uses only standard Transformer
components arranged this way,</font>

53
00:03:38,797 --> 00:03:43,650
<font color="#000000">many earlier geometric networks fall out as special
cases of the framework.</font>

54
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:49,897
<font color="#000000">The authors evaluate GeoMFormer across a broad
suite of tasks that together stress both</font>

55
00:03:49,977 --> 00:03:52,357
<font color="#000000">invariant and equivariant abilities.</font>

56
00:03:52,437 --> 00:03:54,681
<font color="#000000">On the Open Catalyst 2020 dataset,</font>

57
00:03:54,761 --> 00:03:59,534
<font color="#000000">spanning over four hundred sixty thousand
adsorbate-catalyst complexes,</font>

58
00:03:59,614 --> 00:04:05,002
<font color="#000000">they test both the Initial Structure to Relaxed
Energy task, which is invariant,</font>

59
00:04:05,082 --> 00:04:10,060
<font color="#000000">and the Initial Structure to Relaxed Structure
task, which is equivariant.</font>

60
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:14,434
<font color="#000000">On the large quantum chemistry datasets PCQM4Mv2
and Molecule3D,</font>

61
00:04:14,514 --> 00:04:18,946
<font color="#000000">with millions of molecules, they predict the
HOMO-LUMO energy gap.</font>

62
00:04:19,026 --> 00:04:25,849
<font color="#000000">They further use a synthetic five-particle N-body
simulation to test equivariant position prediction,</font>

63
00:04:25,929 --> 00:04:30,633
<font color="#000000">and the MD17 dataset for force-field modeling in
the ablation studies.</font>

64
00:04:30,713 --> 00:04:36,580
<font color="#000000">This breadth lets a single architecture be judged
on both scalar and vector prediction.</font>

65
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:40,759
<font color="#000000">GeoMFormer delivers strong results everywhere it is
tested.</font>

66
00:04:40,839 --> 00:04:46,149
<font color="#000000">On the Open Catalyst energy prediction task it
outperforms prior invariant models,</font>

67
00:04:46,229 --> 00:04:50,817
<font color="#000000">and on the structure prediction task it excels at
equivariant modeling.</font>

68
00:04:50,897 --> 00:04:56,930
<font color="#000000">On the PCQM4Mv2 benchmark it reaches the lowest
error among models with quadratic complexity,</font>

69
00:04:57,010 --> 00:05:01,401
<font color="#000000">a six point seven percent relative reduction over
the previous best,</font>

70
00:05:01,481 --> 00:05:05,148
<font color="#000000">while staying efficient enough to scale to large systems.</font>

71
00:05:05,228 --> 00:05:10,801
<font color="#000000">On Molecule3D it improves error by sixteen point
three percent on the random split and</font>

72
00:05:10,881 --> 00:05:13,891
<font color="#000000">eleven point six percent on the scaffold split.</font>

73
00:05:13,971 --> 00:05:20,662
<font color="#000000">And on the N-body simulation it cuts mean squared
error by a striking thirty-three point eight percent.</font>

74
00:05:20,742 --> 00:05:24,672
<font color="#000000">A single architecture, built from standard
Transformer parts,</font>

75
00:05:24,752 --> 00:05:29,800
<font color="#000000">achieves state-of-the-art performance on both
invariant and equivariant tasks.</font>

76
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:35,186
<font color="#000000">To understand where the gains come from, the
authors ablate each building block.</font>

77
00:05:35,266 --> 00:05:42,307
<font color="#000000">The most telling finding concerns the
cross-attention modules that bridge the invariant and equivariant streams.</font>

78
00:05:42,387 --> 00:05:44,087
<font color="#000000">Removing them hurts sharply.</font>

79
00:05:44,167 --> 00:05:46,313
<font color="#000000">On the MD17 energy prediction task,</font>

80
00:05:46,393 --> 00:05:52,416
<font color="#000000">adding the invariant cross-attention gives an
eighteen point seven percent relative improvement,</font>

81
00:05:52,496 --> 00:05:56,422
<font color="#000000">the equivariant cross-attention gives nine point
eight percent,</font>

82
00:05:56,502 --> 00:06:00,046
<font color="#000000">and using both together gives twenty point eight percent.</font>

83
00:06:00,126 --> 00:06:04,305
<font color="#000000">On the harder MD17 force prediction task the effect
is even larger,</font>

84
00:06:04,385 --> 00:06:10,473
<font color="#000000">with a sixty point eight percent relative
improvement when both cross-attention modules are used.</font>

85
00:06:10,553 --> 00:06:15,686
<font color="#000000">On the N-body simulation the combined improvement
is seventeen point five percent.</font>

86
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:17,784
<font color="#000000">The self-attention, feed-forward,</font>

87
00:06:17,864 --> 00:06:21,154
<font color="#000000">and layer-normalization modules also each contribute,</font>

88
00:06:21,234 --> 00:06:25,350
<font color="#000000">but the cross-attention bridge is clearly the heart
of the design.</font>

89
00:06:25,730 --> 00:06:29,510
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

90
00:06:29,590 --> 00:06:33,370
<font color="#000000">On PCQM4Mv2,</font>

91
00:06:33,450 --> 00:06:37,230
<font color="#000000">GeoMFormer reaches a validation error of zero point
zero seven three four,</font>

92
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:41,090
<font color="#000000">the best of any quadratic-complexity model,</font>

93
00:06:41,170 --> 00:06:44,950
<font color="#000000">a six point seven percent relative reduction.</font>

94
00:06:45,030 --> 00:06:48,810
<font color="#000000">On the N-body simulation it achieves a mean squared
error of zero point zero zero four seven,</font>

95
00:06:48,890 --> 00:06:52,670
<font color="#000000">a thirty-three point eight percent reduction over
the previous best.</font>

96
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:56,530
<font color="#000000">On Molecule3D it records errors of zero point zero
two five two on the random split and</font>

97
00:06:56,610 --> 00:07:00,390
<font color="#000000">zero point one zero four five on the scaffold split,</font>

98
00:07:00,470 --> 00:07:04,250
<font color="#000000">improvements of sixteen point three and eleven
point six percent.</font>

99
00:07:04,330 --> 00:07:08,110
<font color="#000000">And in the ablations,</font>

100
00:07:08,190 --> 00:07:11,970
<font color="#000000">adding cross-attention yields up to a sixty point
eight percent relative improvement on force prediction,</font>

101
00:07:12,050 --> 00:07:15,830
<font color="#000000">underscoring how central that bridge is.</font>

102
00:07:16,210 --> 00:07:20,250
<font color="#000000">The lasting message of this work is that you do not
need bespoke,</font>

103
00:07:20,330 --> 00:07:24,307
<font color="#000000">heuristic modules to model molecules under physical
constraints.</font>

104
00:07:24,387 --> 00:07:27,856
<font color="#000000">By running two standard Transformer streams in parallel,</font>

105
00:07:27,936 --> 00:07:31,089
<font color="#000000">one for invariant and one for equivariant features,</font>

106
00:07:31,169 --> 00:07:34,131
<font color="#000000">and connecting them with simple cross-attention,</font>

107
00:07:34,211 --> 00:07:40,216
<font color="#000000">GeoMFormer learns both kinds of representation at
once and outperforms specialized architectures</font>

108
00:07:40,296 --> 00:07:41,801
<font color="#000000">on a wide range of tasks.</font>

109
00:07:41,881 --> 00:07:45,858
<font color="#000000">Because many earlier models are special cases of
this framework,</font>

110
00:07:45,938 --> 00:07:48,076
<font color="#000000">GeoMFormer offers a clean, general,</font>

111
00:07:48,156 --> 00:07:53,020
<font color="#000000">and scalable design principle for geometric
molecular representation learning.</font>
